# Created 2026-10-09, copied from the sister repository's (`designer-portal`) and extended. Until then
# this repository had none, and that mattered more here than there: the root `.env` holds this
# deployment's production secrets, beside `fieldrepo-release.p12` and `fieldrepo-debug.keystore`.
# All of them are gitignored, but the Vercel CLI does not read .gitignore, and its own default ignore
# list covers `.env.local` and `.env.*.local` but NOT `.env`. So a manual non-prebuilt `vercel
# deploy` from the repository root (an Actions outage is when somebody reaches for one) would upload
# every production secret as a source file of a deployment that everybody in the team can read for
# as long as it exists. CI is not exposed either way: it deploys with `--prebuilt`, which uploads
# only `.vercel/output` (see the last section for the one way this file still reaches that path).
#
# WHY A DEPLOY STARTS AT THE ROOT. The project's Root Directory is `frontend`, so a deploy MUST be
# launched from the repository root (from inside frontend/ Vercel looks for frontend/frontend and
# fails with "No Next.js version detected"). The upload would otherwise also carry the FastAPI
# backend, the Android application, the docs and the CodeGraph index; the sister repository hit
# "Error: Request body too large. Limit: 10mb" that way on 2026-08-23, before any build ran.
#
# DO NOT REWRITE THIS AS AN ALLOWLIST. `/*` followed by `!/frontend/` is the standard gitignore recipe,
# and Vercel's matcher does not honour the re-include: the sister repository tried it and the deploy
# died on "The specified Root Directory "frontend" does not exist". A denylist goes stale when somebody
# adds a heavy top-level directory, so if a deploy fails on the 10 MB limit, name the new directory
# here rather than reaching for the negation form.
#
# THE CLI NEVER READS .gitignore, WITH OR WITHOUT THIS FILE. Vercel CLI 59.5.0 builds its upload
# ignore set from a built-in list plus this file (or a `.nowignore`) and nothing else, so build and
# dev output has to be named below even though git ignores it. Deleting this file would therefore
# not hand the job back to .gitignore: only the built-in list would apply (`.git`, `.vercel`, `.next`,
# `node_modules`, `.env.local`, `.env.*.local` and a few more), which excludes neither `.env` nor the
# keystores named above, nor any of the directories below.
#
# `next build` under Root Directory `frontend` reads nothing outside frontend/ (checked 2026-10-09:
# no import, config file or package script reaches a parent path). `shared/` is left in
# deliberately: only test specs read it, it is a few kilobytes, and excluding a directory a future
# import might reach is the more expensive mistake.

# The other products in this repository. Nothing here is read by `next build`.
/backend/
/android/
/docker/
/infra/

# Prose, tooling and agent configuration.
/docs/
/scripts/
/.github/
/.claude/
/.agents/
*.docx
/README.md
/KEYSTORE-CUSTODY.md

# Generated locally, never an input to a build.
/.codegraph/
/.ruff_cache/
/test-results/
/node_modules/
/frontend/node_modules/
/frontend/.next/
/frontend/out/
/frontend/coverage/
/frontend/playwright-report/
/frontend/test-results/

# Secrets and key material never travel in a deployment payload; Vercel supplies the environment.
#
# `/.env` AND `/.env.*` ARE ANCHORED, AND THAT IS NOT STYLE. An unanchored `.env.*` matches a basename
# at any depth, so it would also exclude `frontend/.env.example` and `frontend/.env.local.example`,
# committed templates with no secret in them. The Next.js builder attaches every `.env` and `.env.*`
# file in the ROOT DIRECTORY (frontend/) to every function (checked against the source of Vercel CLI
# 59.5.0 and its bundled @vercel/next 6.0.0 on 2026-10-09), and on a `--prebuilt` deploy a function
# file this list matches is not uploaded, so Vercel then fails on the missing file. The sister
# repository's CI deploys failed exactly that way on 2026-08-23 with
# `ENOENT: no such file or directory, lstat '/vercel/path0/frontend/.env.example'`. The builder reads
# no other directory for them, so anchored root patterns cannot cause it.
#
# The key-material patterns are unanchored on purpose: a keystore, a private key or Terraform state is
# never a build input, wherever it sits. None of them matches a file under frontend/ outside
# node_modules/, and a function file under node_modules/ is uploaded whatever this file says.
/.env
/.env.*
.env.local
.env.*.local
*.p12
*.jks
*.keystore
*.pem
*.tfstate*
